so arckhipov didn't not trust the computers, he just didn't want to launch without official ordersFeb 21Right, exactly. It's a crucial distinction. Arkhipov's objection wasn't philosophical or technological — it was procedural and practical.
His argument was essentially: we don't actually know what's happening up there. The depth charges might be signals, not an attack. We've lost contact with Moscow. We have no confirmation that war has actually started. So launching a nuclear torpedo based on assumptions and panic would be reckless.
He was advocating for the basic military principle of not acting without orders or confirmed intelligence. Surface, reestablish communications, find out what's actually going on. Which is exactly what happened — they surfaced and discovered that, no, war had not broken out.
So it's really quite different from the AM scenario in "I Have No Mouth." Ellison's story is about a malevolent artificial intelligence that gains autonomy and turns on humanity — a fundamentally different anxiety. 


