so for each character there's a literrally reference made except for Nimdok and I like this exercise so I'm gonna say... Kafka. The Trial, specifically. A man thrust into a system of judgment where the rules are opaque, the charges unclear, and guilt is assumed before it's even articulated. Nimdok enters not remembering what he did, but the scenario assumes his guilt from the first frame. Everything is structured around a verdict that's already been reached. He just has to discover what he's guilty of.
And there's a deeper Kafka connection — Kafka was a Prague Jew writing about institutional machinery that processes people without regard for their humanity. Nimdok was part of that machinery. So putting Nimdok in a Kafka scenario would be forcing the architect to experience the architecture from the inside.
But I'm not sure that's right either. Kafka's horror is bureaucratic absurdity — guilt without crime. Nimdok's horror is the opposite. The crime is real. The guilt is earned. There's nothing absurd about it.
Maybe the better connection is Primo Levi. If This Is a Man. Levi wrote about the concentration camps from the inside, as a survivor, and his central obsession was the grey zone — the space where victims were forced into complicity, where the line between perpetrator and prisoner blurred. Nimdok's scenario does exactly that. AM doesn't just show him what he did — it gives him the chance to do it again. The question isn't "do you remember?" It's "are you still that person?"
And Levi's own end haunts it. He likely died by suicide in 1987, decades after surviving Auschwitz. The memory didn't kill him in the camp. It killed him after, slowly. That's what AM is betting on with Nimdok — that remembering won't break him in the moment but will prove that the person who did those things is still in there, waiting.
Or Dostoevsky. The Grand Inquisitor from Brothers Karamazov. A man who believes his cruelty serves a higher purpose, who built a rational framework for inflicting suffering. That's Mengele's world, which is Nimdok's world. The Inquisitor tells Christ himself that freedom is too much for people, that they need to be managed, controlled, even hurt — for their own good. Nimdok's Nazi science had that same architecture of justification.
Same distance as the others. Same quality of "yeah, you can see it, but it's not that.
